Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.640 --> 00:00:02.869 align:start position:0%
 
moscow says it will increase missile  attacks on kiev the warning follows  unverified russian claims that ukraine  carried out airstrikes on villages in  russian territory and sank russia's  largest warships in the blank sea in the  black sea we're now getting reports of  fatalities following russian strikes in  eastern ukraine as the war passes its  50th day air raid sirens have been  sounding across the country  days into the war and this is still the  wake up call in kiev  heavy explosions were heard here  overnight they're said to be the most  powerful since russian forces pulled out  of the region around the capital  says it will step up its attacks  tonight sea-based high-precision  long-range missiles hit a military  facility on the outskirts of kiev the  number and scale of missile strikes  against objects in kiev will increase in  response to any attacks of a terrorist  nature or sabotage on russian territory  by the kiev nationalist regime  some are asking if it's also in  retaliation for the sinking of this  warship the moskva russia's acknowledged  the ship has been lost but not that it  was due to ukrainian missiles either way  a big blow according to the pentagon  this is a cruiser they only have three  in this class and it's basically  designed for air defense that's uh  that's what this ship is designed to do  not unlike our own cruisers  so it's going to have an impact on their  capabilities certainly in the near term  but this footage from the southern port  city of mariupol serves as a reminder of  moscow's military might and brutality  with russia now focusing its attention  on ukraine's east mariupol would be key  in connecting the southern and eastern  fronts  despite a strong russian presence  ukraine says the city hasn't fallen yet  the country's president used his nightly  address to praise ukrainian resilience  to put it mildly no one was sure that we  would survive  everyone sympathized  many advised us to leave the  country advised us to actually surrender  to tyranny  but they didn't know us either  and they did not know how brave  ukrainians are  how much we value freedom  our opportunity to live the way we want  this war continues and ukrainians will  need to show even more of that bravery  if they're to repel the imminent russian  onslaught in the  east dw correspondent rebecca ritters is  in butcher which ukraine recently  recaptured from russian forces i asked  her about the situation there now  i'm here in that town that has now been  liberated  i want i mean it's definitely nothing  resembling normal phil here it's a ghost  town but uh one thing i was really  surprised by something that really  struck me was just the pace at which  everything has been cleared away you  know the world by now the world has  pretty much seen those horrific images  when peop  ukraine finally gained access to this  area again of all the tanks and the  debris and  stuff in that residential block that  everyone uh was seeing  all of that pretty much has now been  cleared it was really uh  quite surprising i was surprised i've  only been in kiev one day myself so i  didn't see any of it previously but  definitely everyone here just really  trying hard to get this city back on its  feet some of the people we spoke to  definitely telling me of the atrocities  that took place here and showing me  welcoming me into their homes which have  been all but destroyed one gentleman  whose house had been been used as a  russian headquarters and his house had  been completely destroyed the russians  blew it up before they left or just  after they left i suppose uh so it's  gonna take a very long time you'll see  behind me everything all the shops  completely still destroyed it's going to  take a very long time but people doing  what they can to get this back on its  feet  and and how are authorities going about  gathering evidence  yeah well that is the next step i mean  at the moment authorities are working  hard to gather evidence of war crimes so  far the human rights commission of  ukraine as of today has over 6 000  documented cases um authorities  definitely going through to talk to  people hundreds if not thousands of  journalists as well have been through  and spoken to people uh organizations  such as human rights watch and and the  like um that is sort of the next phase  at the moment here also so much is being  concentrated on the humanitarian effort  i mean the city is still cut off from  power and water  and telecommunications have been  restored now but obviously that is the  first step and then of course comes the  documentation of of all the war crimes  yesterday i was speaking to the  commissioner or the ombudsman rather for  the human rights commission and she was  saying they've also set up hotlines so  that people can report she said that was  really important particularly in the  cases of the sexual violence claims  because many people were still terrified  and traumatized and found it very  difficult to come forward so there are  hotlines also set up to be able to take  testimony  russian forces are now now seem to be  focusing their attention on the east of  the country um bring us up to data with  what's happening there  well that's right uh heavy bombardment  or refocus of russian efforts is  expected really any day now in the east  uh so obviously for the last week or so  authorities have been telling residents  to just get out of that area while they  still can  i was reporting from down near near that  area on the region bordering donbass on  the weekend spoke to lots of people from  luhansk donates those areas in the  donbass mass evacuations going on from  the east and the south but not everybody  is leaving of course as of the other day  still around about 30 percent of people  were staying in luhansk i actually spoke  to a gentleman at the train station when  i was leaving that area and he he was  leaving too but he was telling me uh  about some friends of his in lahars that  simply didn't want to leave they were  going to stick around and try and defend  they said they've lost everything  already in 2014 they weren't prepared to  do it again and he broke down in tears  as he was telling me the story obviously  the scenes that you just saw in that  report there from mario paul the scenes  that we've seen here from places such as  butcher everyone worried that we're  going to see similar scenes like that  down in the east  that's rebecca written in butcher well  ukraine's plight has prompted offers of  help from around the world but when  asked what they need most the answer  from the ukrainian leadership is always  the same  let's get more on this from liam collins  he's a retired u.s special forces  colonel and has been a military adviser  to ukraine he's now executive director  of the madison policy forum and joins us  from west point in the united states so  welcome to dw um all these weapons are  pouring into ukraine but how long is it  is it going to take for them to become  useful for ukrainian soldiers to be  trained and able to use them to good  effect  it really depends on the weapon system  and what the capability is for example  the list of most recent aid to ukraine  included what i saw from the us mi-17  helicopters which are russian  helicopters that their pilots are more  than capable of flying  so that's something they can integrate  right away javelin weapon systems we  started giving those in january of uh  2018 so again that's a weapon you can  disseminate and immediately employ the  switchblade uh drones you just spoke  about those we've been training them on  for the last few weeks and so we have a  cadre that's trained but obviously not  everybody out in the field so that will  take time so it really depends on the  specific weapon system and what the  training time is right and so it looks  like ukraine is not going to be short of  hardware supplies how is it doing in  terms of actual troops to operate these  weapons  yeah i mean that really hasn't you know  seen a lot of news on that in terms of  what losses they've taken i mean it's  they're fighting overwhelming combat  power by the russians and they're slowly  getting attributed so we have to  constantly place those weapon systems  the ammunition that they're that they're  exhausting uh but right the the fighters  are gonna be harder to replace but they  do have tens of thousands of right  volunteers in their territorial defense  force uh that that can supplement that  effort so  it looks like they've been able to  maintain that manpower for the time  being um and they just got to continue  to do that  and what are you hearing about how  russia is doing in terms of rearming and  putting itself in a position to keep up  this pressure we do hear a lot about  their hardware being defective and badly  maintained but they do seem to be in a  bind they've got millions of dollars  coming in every day from gas exports but  sanctions mean a dwindling number of  sources of technology to maintain and  replenish their hardware  i mean for the most part right those gas  exports and those sanctions those are  more longer-term effects they're not  going to affect the immediate  warfighting capability uh it's really  russia has a large amount stockpiled so  it's just getting those stockpiles to  the fight and as you said some are  dilapidated they haven't been maintained  some of those weapon systems they don't  have a good logistical support system  and we've seen throughout this war and  so now they're right trying to  reposition troops that have already been  uh you know some have been beat down  pretty badly up up in kiev and harkee  and they're trying to reposition them to  the east so it's it's gonna be uh you  know a challenge to put good uh capable  units uh into the fight on the east that  haven't taken uh significant action  already  let's talk about uh this because we have  this is what we've been hearing now that  that russia is preparing to go again at  ukraine uh ukraine's east and south how  do you see that panning out  yeah i mean obviously  putin's trying to salvage some kind of  victory and he knows he can't get it up  in the north so he's trying to  consolidate his forces and see what he  can pull out of this and and so i expect  to see it play out similar to what we've  seen to date right you've got the  overwhelming combat power of russia  against a much more tactically competent  uh ukrainian armed forces right that has  good leaders well-trained professional  forces that are going to beat them in  the tactical fight so it's just a matter  of can russia a trip uh you know the  more capable force to a great enough  level to attack allow him to achieve  victory or can ukraine continue to hold  off you know the the russian horde with  smart smartly time you know good  defenses and and smart time counter  attacks that they can do it to their  advantage  and a brief word about mario paul  how bad a loss would it be if uh for  ukraine if were russia to take that uh  port city  yeah i mean it's not a strategic  capability i mean  in not not in times of war right it's a  key trading port they need it for that  capability but i mean they haven't had  that since the war began and so it yeah  i mean  we heard about the bridge between uh uh  crimea and the east but it's it's not as  strategic as people are making it out to  be ukraine cannot right initially right  defended all their territory they were  going to take losses early in the war in  terms of territory that they have to  fight back as they did up in the north  and kiev and harkee so that's what i  would expect to see there and and russia  still despite his efforts down there has  not been able to take control of it at  this point okay good talking to you  thank you so much for joining us colonel  liam collins from the madison policy  forum

